os-test in V86BSD
=================

This directory is Sortix's os-test (https://gitlab.com/sortix/os-test),
git 8e790a4 of 19 October 2026, ISC licence (LICENSE), copied unchanged.
V86BSD runs it with lib/libc/tools/ost/ (POSIX sh and c17; see the
README.md there): sh lib/libc/tools/ost/vmrun.sh OBJDIR WORKDIR builds
and runs every suite on V86BSD's own kernel in QEMU and makes the report.

os-test tests POSIX.1-2024.  V86BSD also keeps the interfaces that the
earlier editions had and later ones removed, back to the Single UNIX
Specification, Version 2 (share/doc/legacy.md), and tests them here, in
os-test's own form, so that the report shows them beside the rest and
beside other systems' results:

  legacy/          the declarations of SUSv2 that POSIX.1-2024 no longer
                   has, one .api file per header in os-test's .api
                   language (written from the text of SUSv2, SUSv3 and
                   SUSv4), and gen.sh, which makes legacy-include from
                   them (posix-parse only reads the 2024 HTML)
  legacy-include/  a suite like include: one compile test per
                   declaration, compiled with -D_XOPEN_SOURCE=500
  misc/try-compile-legacy.sh
                   misc/try-compile.sh for legacy-include: -D_XOPEN_SOURCE
                   =500 in place of the 2024 macros
  xcurses/         the declarations of X/Open Curses, Issue 4, Version 2
                   (SUSv2's curses), one .api file per header, made with
                   "sh legacy/gen.sh xcurses xcurses-include"
  xcurses-include/ a suite like legacy-include for <curses.h>, <term.h>
                   and <unctrl.h>
  misc/try-compile-xcurses.sh
                   misc/try-compile-legacy.sh for xcurses-include, with
                   -D_XOPEN_SOURCE_EXTENDED=1 and -lcurses

What else was changed in os-test's own files: misc/suites.list has the
new suites at its end, and .gitignore lets git see legacy/,
legacy-include/, xcurses/ and xcurses-include/.  Nothing else.

On another system (glibc on Linux, say) the legacy suites run as the
others do: "make" in the suite's directory, or the whole of os-test with
its makefiles; out/<os>/ then holds that system's results for the
report.
